**TL;DR**


The` @mastra/browser-firecrawl` package gives a Mastra agent a hosted browser for pages behind a login. It keeps the same` AgentBrowser` tools and refs, gives each thread its own session, uses named profiles to stay signed in, and needs no Chromium on your machine.


---


Search and scrape work when the page is public. The vendor portal your ops team logs into every morning, or the report that only exists after you pick a date range and click Export, needs something that can type and click, like a[browser agent](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/best-browser-agents) . Open the inventory page of[Swag Labs](https://www.saucedemo.com/) , Sauce Labs' public demo store, without logging in and you get this:


```text
Epic sadface: You can only access '/inventory.html' when you are logged in.
```


The inventory is there, behind a form, and nothing comes back until something has typed, clicked, and waited. Mastra agents have a` browser` field for that job.` FirecrawlBrowser` , from` @mastra/browser-firecrawl` , runs the browser in a[Firecrawl Interact](https://www.firecrawl.dev/interact) session instead of a Chromium install on your machine, so there is nothing local to keep alive.


Swag Labs stands in for whatever internal tool you need the agent to use.


## How to add a browser to a Mastra agent


A browser attaches to a Mastra agent the way tools and memory do:


```text
// src/mastra/agents/portal-agent.ts
import   { Agent }   from   "@mastra/core/agent"  ;
import   { Memory }   from   "@mastra/memory"  ;
import   { FirecrawlBrowser }   from   "@mastra/browser-firecrawl"  ;


const   browser   =   new   FirecrawlBrowser  ({
apiKey  :   process  .  env  .  FIRECRAWL_API_KEY  ,
});


export   const   portalAgent   =   new   Agent  ({
id  :   "portal-agent"  ,
name  :   "Portal Agent"  ,
model  :   "openai/gpt-5.6-sol"  ,
browser  ,
memory  :   new   Memory  ()  ,
defaultOptions  :   { maxSteps  :   40   }  ,
instructions  :   `You operate a web portal on the user's behalf.
Always call browser_snapshot before acting so you have current element refs.
Use the refs (@e1, @e2, ...) to type and click.
After every action, snapshot again and confirm the page changed the way you expected.
Never guess a ref from a previous page.
Snapshots list interactive elements only. Pass interactiveOnly: false when you need to read page text.`  ,
});
```


The agent now has the 16 browser tools` AgentBrowser` exposes by default:` browser_goto` ,` browser_snapshot` ,` browser_click` ,` browser_type` , and the rest.` FirecrawlBrowser` extends` AgentBrowser` , so the model drives the page the same way. The page itself runs remotely, in a session Mastra requests from the Firecrawl API and drives over CDP.


` Memory` holds the conversation, so "now check out" on turn two resolves against turn one. The browser session holds the cart. Both key on the thread you pass in` memory: { thread, resource }` , so they line up without extra wiring.


Mastra stops an agent after five steps by default, and the login-to-checkout run below took around twenty-five because every action gets its own snapshot. Setting` maxSteps` on the agent means callers don't have to pass it.


## How the agent logs in and clicks: snapshots and refs


The prompt:


> Log in to[https://www.saucedemo.com](https://www.saucedemo.com/) with the username` standard_user` and password` secret_sauce` . Add the Sauce Labs Backpack to the cart, check out as Jane Doe, postal code 94105, and tell me the order confirmation message.


Those are the demo logins Sauce Labs prints on its own login page. For a real portal,[keep credentials out of the prompt](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/best-ai-agent-authentication-platforms) and use asaved profile instead.


The model's first move is` browser_goto` , then` browser_snapshot` . Instead of HTML, it gets the interactive elements of the page with refs. On the login page that is the whole snapshot:


```text
- textbox "Username" @e1
- textbox "Password" @e2
- button "Login" @e3
```


The model calls` browser_type` on` @e1` and` @e2` ,` browser_click` on` @e3` , and snapshots again. Now the tree is the inventory page and the refs are new, because the page is new. Every product there has its own "Add to cart" button, so the model picks the one that follows the Backpack row. From there it's the same loop through cart, checkout form, Continue and Finish.


A ref is only valid for the snapshot that produced it. If the agent gets bounced from` /inventory.html` back to the login page, that page has a "Dismiss error" button at` @e3` and Login moves to` @e4` . That is why the instructions tell the model never to guess a ref from a previous page.


On the confirmation page the default snapshot lists the buttons and footer links and leaves out the message, because the message is plain text. Following the last line of its instructions, the model snapshots again with` interactiveOnly: false` and gets the line the user asked for:


```text
- heading "Thank you for your order!" @e3 [level=2]
- text: Your order has been dispatched, and will arrive just as fast as the pony can get there!
```


The agent returns that sentence without any hand-written selectors or page-specific code.


## One browser session per thread, so users stay separate


` FirecrawlBrowser` defaults to` scope: 'thread'` , the same option` AgentBrowser` has:


- The first time a Mastra thread touches a browser tool,` FirecrawlBrowser` creates a Firecrawl browser session for that thread.
- Later browser calls in that thread reuse the session while it is alive, so the login from turn one still holds on turn three.
- When the thread's browser closes, the remote session is deleted.


The thread is whatever you pass at the call site:


```text
await   portalAgent  .generate  (prompt  ,   {
memory  :   { thread  :   "ops-alice"  ,   resource  :   "alice"   }  ,
});
```


Two users running the portal agent under different thread ids get two isolated browsers with two separate logins, and neither can see the other's cart. Leave out the thread id and every caller shares one default session and one login. If you want one browser for everything on purpose, pass` scope: 'shared'` and Mastra creates one session at launch and shares it until it closes.


Firecrawl closes a session after` ttl` seconds of total lifetime (default 600, max 3600) or` activityTtl` seconds idle (default 300). A thread that comes back after lunch gets a fresh session with no cookies, and the agent is back at the login form.


## How to keep a Mastra agent logged in between runs


Threads keep users apart within a run. To stay logged in across runs, use a[named Firecrawl profile](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/firecrawl-interact-endpoint) , passed under the` firecrawl` key:


```text
const   browser   =   new   FirecrawlBrowser  ({
firecrawl  :   {
profile  :   { name  :   "swag-labs-ops"  ,   saveChanges  :   true   }  ,
ttl  :   600  ,
activityTtl  :   120  ,
}  ,
});
```


` saveChanges: true` writes cookies and login state back to the profile when the session closes. The next session opened with` swag-labs-ops` starts already logged in, so the agent can go straight to` /inventory.html` . Firecrawl writes state when the session closes, so end the login run by closing the browser, either the agent calling` browser_close` or your code calling` browser.closeThreadSession(threadId)` .


Two details before you ship this:


1. **The nested` firecrawl.profile` is not the top-level` profile` .** The top-level one comes from` AgentBrowser` and points at a local Playwright user-data directory. The nested one is stored on Firecrawl's side, and it's the one you want for a hosted browser.
2. **Only one session can save to a profile at a time.** A second session opening the same profile with` saveChanges: true` is refused with a 409:` Browser operation conflicts with the current session or profile state.` With` scope: 'thread'` and several threads in flight, that will bite. Log in once with` saveChanges: true` . Run normal traffic with` saveChanges: false` . Every thread reads the saved login and none of them fight over the write.


```text
// After a one-time login run has saved the profile:
const   browser   =   new   FirecrawlBrowser  ({
firecrawl  :   {
profile  :   { name  :   "swag-labs-ops"  ,   saveChanges  :   false   }  ,
}  ,
});
```


Every thread that opens` swag-labs-ops` is signed in as the same account, which is what you want for a shared ops login. If each user has their own account on the portal, give each user their own profile, for example` portal-${userId}` , and log each one in once the same way.


Profiles also keep credentials out of the prompt. A person or a setup script types the password once, into the session that saves the profile, and the agent only ever opens the profile, so the password never reaches the model, the traces, or memory. The content of the logged-in pages the agent reads still goes to the model and the traces, like any other tool output.


## How to install FirecrawlBrowser


The Firecrawl team built the provider and it lives in the Mastra monorepo. In a new or existing[Mastra project](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/mastra-tutorial) :


```text
npm   install   @mastra/core   @mastra/memory   @mastra/libsql   @mastra/browser-firecrawl   agent-browser@0.19.0
```


Set` FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` , plus` OPENAI_API_KEY` for the` openai/...` model string above (or the key for whichever provider you pick).` Memory` needs a storage provider on your` Mastra` instance. The` create-mastra` scaffold already has one; otherwise register the agent with LibSQL:


```text
// src/mastra/index.ts
import   { Mastra }   from   "@mastra/core/mastra"  ;
import   { LibSQLStore }   from   "@mastra/libsql"  ;
import   { portalAgent }   from   "./agents/portal-agent"  ;


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
agents  :   { portalAgent }  ,
storage  :   new   LibSQLStore  ({ id  :   "mastra-storage"  ,   url  :   "file:./mastra.db"   })  ,
});
```


Then run the prompt from earlier, and close the thread's browser when you're done so the Firecrawl session doesn't sit open until its TTL:


```text
// run.ts
import   { mastra }   from   "./src/mastra"  ;


const   agent   =   mastra  .getAgent  (  "portalAgent"  );
const   thread   =   "ops-alice"  ;


try   {
const   result   =   await   agent  .generate  (
"Log in to https://www.saucedemo.com with the username `standard_user` and password `secret_sauce`. Add the Sauce Labs Backpack to the cart, check out as Jane Doe, postal code 94105, and tell me the order confirmation message."  ,
{ memory  :   { thread  ,   resource  :   "alice"   } }  ,
);
console  .log  (  result  .text);
}   finally   {
await   agent  .  browser  ?.closeThreadSession  (thread);
}
```


Send one request at a time per thread. The same Firecrawl key covers[Search](https://docs.firecrawl.dev/features/search) and[Scrape](https://docs.firecrawl.dev/features/scrape) if the agent needs public pages too.


The[FirecrawlBrowser reference](https://mastra.ai/reference/browser/firecrawl-browser) has every option, the[browser overview](https://mastra.ai/docs/browser) covers providers and screencast, and the[Firecrawl browser docs](https://docs.firecrawl.dev/features/browser) cover sessions and profiles. For the short version of the setup, see the[Firecrawl + Mastra integration page](https://www.firecrawl.dev/integrations/mastra) .
